Article 6
(1) The obligations of Regional Government in efforts to protect women include:
a. establishing and implementing policy programs and activities for the protection of women's rights;
b. conducting cooperation with the community and/or Community Organizations in the implementation of women's protection in accordance with laws and regulations;
c. conducting rehabilitation and empowerment of women who are victims of acts of violence;
2021, No. 3 -80-
d. providing special protection to women victims of acts of violence and human trafficking; and
e. taking preventive action in order to prevent violations of women's rights.
(2) The obligations of Regional Government in efforts to protect children include:
a. being responsible for respecting the fulfillment of children's rights without distinguishing tribe, religion, race, group, gender, ethnicity, culture and language, legal status, birth order, and physical and/or mental condition;
b. having the obligation and responsibility to implement and support national policy in the implementation of child protection in the region;
c. the policy as referred to in letter b can be realized through regional efforts to build child-friendly regencies/cities;
d. having the obligation and responsibility to provide support for facilities, infrastructure, and availability of human resources in the implementation of child protection;
e. guaranteeing the protection, care, and welfare of children by taking into account the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, or other persons who are legally responsible for the child;
f. supervising the implementation of child protection.
g. guaranteeing children to use their rights in expressing opinions in accordance with the age and level of intelligence of the child.

Article 14
Household neglect as referred to in Article 10 letter d is caused by:
a. acts that result in the failure to fulfill the needs of a child properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after a child as appropriate, committed by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;
c. acts that neglect a person within the household scope, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to an agreement or contract they are obligated to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/or
d. acts that result in economic dependency by means of restricting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

MAYOR OF PADANG SIDEMPUAN
NORTH SUMATRA PROVINCE
REGIONAL REGULATION OF PADANG SIDEMPUAN CITY NUMBER 3 OF 2021
CONCERNING
WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION
BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD
THE MAYOR OF PADANG SIDEMPUAN,

Considering a. that women and children are creatures of Almighty God who have human rights guaranteed by the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia, therefore women and children must be protected from all forms of violence and discrimination;

b. that violence against women and children in the Region tends to increase, therefore the Regional Government and/or community need to play an active role optimally to provide protection, so that women and children are prevented and free from violence or threats of violence, torture, or treatment that degrades human dignity and honor;

c. that protection of women and children is a mandatory concurrent affair which is the authority, obligation, and responsibility of the Regional Government, thus requiring regulation as a basis for implementing comprehensive and integrated protection of women and children;

d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in Padang Sidempuan City;

In View of 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

